course: dictionary
lesson: 13153
title: Forfeiture: losing something as a punishment
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Forfeiture is a long, formal word, but its meaning is simple: you lose something because you broke a rule or a law. The thing you lose might be money, property or a right. «The judge ordered the forfeiture of the money the thief had gained from crime.» The thief broke the law, so the money is taken away as a punishment.
explain: Look at the words around it to find the meaning. Here, the clues are «judge», «thief» and «crime». A judge works in a court, and a court decides on punishments for crime. So forfeiture must be a punishment that takes something away. Swap in your meaning, "the taking away of the money as a punishment", and check that it still makes sense.
explain: In sport, forfeiture has a different but related meaning. «Our team got a forfeiture because they did not turn up for the match.» Here the team loses the match because it broke the rules by not arriving. The word still means losing something, but this time the thing lost is the game.
fact: Forfeiture is a noun, and it means losing property, money or a right as a punishment.
fact: «Forfeiture» is often used in law: «asset forfeiture» means the police take money or things that were gained from crime.
fact: In sport, forfeiture means losing a match because a team broke the rules or did not turn up.
fact: «Forfeiture of a deposit» means you lose the money you paid in advance, usually because you broke the agreement.
fact: The verb related to this word is «forfeit», which means to lose something because you broke a rule.
exercise: «The judge ordered the forfeiture of the money the thief had gained from crime.» What does forfeiture mean here? | Buying a new house with the money | Winning a prize for being clever | Giving the money to charity | *Losing money or things as a punishment for breaking the law
exercise: «Our team got a forfeiture because they did not turn up for the match.» In sport, what does forfeiture mean? | Playing the match again the next day | *Losing the match because the team broke the rules or did not turn up | Winning the match by a big score | Getting extra time to practise
exercise: Which phrase means the police taking money or things gained from crime? | a book of rules | a deposit of cash | *asset forfeiture | a sports match
test: What is forfeiture? | Finding something that was lost | Being given a medal for hard work | *Losing money, property or a right as a punishment for breaking a rule or law | Making a brand new rule for the class
test: Which example shows forfeiture in sport? | A team trains for an extra hour after school | A team wins a match by scoring two goals | *A team loses the match because it broke the rules | A team changes the colour of its kit
exam: «The court ordered forfeiture of the stolen car.» What does forfeiture mean in this sentence? | Painting the car a new colour | *Taking the car away as a punishment for the crime | Returning the car to its owner with an apology | Lending the car to a friend
